This project classifies iris flowers into three species—Setosa, Versicolor, and Virginica—using two popular machine learning models: K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) and Decision Tree. 

The Iris dataset, which contains four key measurements for each flower, is loaded and split into training and testing sets. 

Features are scaled for KNN to ensure fair distance comparisons. Both models are trained on the same data and evaluated using accuracy, confusion matrices 

(printed via SymPy for clarity), and classification reports including precision, recall, and F1-score. 

The results are then compared to determine which model performs better. 

This project provides a complete beginner-friendly example of supervised learning and model evaluation.
